  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nA weak frontal boundary is draped across the region today. The \npersistent showers that have been ongoing across the north half of \nthe region are finally beginning to shrivel up. Meanwhile, isolated \nconvection has developed mainly across west Kentucky where the \nhighest instability is. A shortwave aloft to our west will slowly \npivot eastward and provide additional lift by this evening for more \nwidespread showers and storms to develop. The environment in place \nis certainly capable of at least a few severe storms with deep layer \nshear of 30-35 kts and MLCAPE of 2000-3000 j/kg. Mid level lapse \nrates are poor and freezing levels are high, so damaging winds \nshould be the primary concern with any of the stronger storms. \nCoverage of storms is anticipated to increase as we get into the \nearly evening timeframe. The window of concern for severe storms may \ncontinue into mid or late evening (03-04z) before subsiding.\n\nA mid level disturbance will slowly shift eastward across the Ozark \nPlateau and lower Ohio Valley Friday into Saturday, providing a \nfocus for at least scattered showers and storms each day. Looks like \nthe threat may be more confined to just west Kentucky and possibly \nsouthwest Indiana on Saturday. Shear and instability are weaker so \nnot expecting any severe storms the next couple of days.\n\nUpper level ridging builds back into the region Sunday and remains \nin place through mid next week. 90 degree temperatures are likely \n(60-80% chance via the LREF) across the southwest half of the area \non Sunday and the entire area Monday through Wednesday. Perhaps we \ncan finally get some cooler weather by the later next week or the \nweekend of September 19-20. Guidance has been waffling back and \nforth whether to bring a true shot of cooler air (highs in the 70s) \nor just bring us closer to normal in the low to mid 80s by that time.",
